>
  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="checks">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>What should I check first?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">These checks take a few minutes and fix many shower problems.</p>
      <ol class="sg-steps">
        <li><strong>Clean the shower head.</strong> Unscrew it and soak it in descaler or white vinegar for an hour. Limescale is the most common cause of a weak shower.</li>
        <li><strong>Check the other hot taps.</strong> If no hot tap works, the problem is the boiler or hot water, not the shower. See <a href="/no-heating-no-hot-water-liverpool">no heating or hot water</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Check the isolation valve.</strong> Many showers have a small valve on the pipe, often with a screwdriver slot. Make sure it is fully open.</li>
        <li><strong>Electric shower?</strong> Check the pull-cord switch is on, and that nothing has tripped at the fuse box.</li>
        <li><strong>Power shower?</strong> Listen for the pump. If it hums but no water comes, or makes no sound at all, the pump needs checking.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="brx-note"><p><strong>Electric shower tripping the power?</strong> Stop using it. Do not keep resetting the fuse box. Call us, and our partner electricians can check the circuit if needed.</p></div>
      <div class="brx-cta">
        <p><strong>Tried the checks?</strong> Tell us what the shower is doing and we will give you a price.</p>
        <a href={TEL} class="brx-btn">Call {PHONE}</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="faults">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>What is wrong with my shower?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">What the shower is doing points to the likely fault.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table">
          <thead><tr><th>What it does</th><th>Likely cause</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Goes hot and cold</strong></td><td>A worn thermostatic cartridge, or low water pressure on a combi</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Weak flow</strong></td><td>Limescale in the head or hose, or a part-closed valve</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>No hot water</strong></td><td>The boiler or hot water system, or a failed cartridge</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Electric shower cuts out</strong></td><td>The safety cut-out, from low flow, limescale or a worn part</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Electric shower trips the power</strong></td><td>A failed heater or water inside the unit. Stop using it</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Drips when off</strong></td><td>A worn seal or cartridge in the valve</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Leaks through the ceiling</strong></td><td>A leaking valve, waste, tray or seal. Stop using it</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Power shower pump noisy or dead</strong></td><td>A worn or failed pump, or air in the pump</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="call">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>When should I call you?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">If the checks have not helped, or straight away if water is leaking or the shower trips the power.</p>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li>Water is coming through a ceiling</li>
        <li>An electric shower trips the fuse box or smells of burning</li>
        <li>The shower goes scalding hot without warning</li>
        <li>It drips all the time when turned off</li>
        <li>The pump will not run</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="cost">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How much does a shower repair cost?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">The first hour is £120 on weekdays, 9am to 4pm. There is no call-out fee. Parts are priced before we fit them.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Job</th><th>Price inc VAT</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Shower repair, weekdays 9am to 4pm</strong></td><td>First hour £120</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Shower repair, evenings, nights and weekends</strong></td><td>First hour £150</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong><a href="/showers-liverpool">New electric shower, supplied and fitted</a></strong></td><td>From £375</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong><a href="/showers-liverpool">Thermostatic bar mixer, supplied and fitted</a></strong></td><td>From £395</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong><a href="/showers-liverpool">Shower fitted, you supply it</a></strong></td><td>From £195</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>See our full <a href="/plumber-prices-liverpool">price list</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="replace">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Should I repair or replace my shower?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Many faults can be fixed. We look first and give you an honest answer before any work.</p>
      <p>A new cartridge, seal or hose often fixes a mixer shower. Electric showers often last 5 to 10 years. If yours is over 10 years old and has a fault, a new one is often better value than a repair.</p>
      <p>If you decide on a new shower, see our <a href="/showers-liverpool">shower installation</a> page for prices and options.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</ServiceGuide>
